Mobile augmented reality applications are gaining traction due to their expanding scope of services, however, this expansion leads to significant latency and mobility-related issues. To tackle these problems, we introduce a service decomposition framework that optimizes application performance. By dividing complex AR services into manageable, independent components, we can individually prioritize and offload the most computationally intensive parts to the cloud for processing. As a result, our approach yields substantial improvements in system resource utilization, application quality, and energy efficiency. Additionally, our framework dynamically adjusts AR services to match users' preferences and current network conditions. Experimental results, based on a real-world implementation of an AR-based indoor navigation system, confirm the effectiveness of our framework in delivering high-quality AR experiences with minimal latency. The outcomes also highlight the significance of considering users' preferences in AR service design. Notably, our framework achieves a 2.2 times improvement in energy efficiency and a 25% decrease in energy waste compared to other mobile cloud computing strategies. Finally, our framework's adaptability and scalability are validated through a set of experiments that analyze the impact of various parameters and mobile devices on system performance. The source code of our framework is freely available for download from a GitHub repository. https://github.com/abdelrahman-mohamed/ServiceDecomposition. The associated video demonstration is accessible at https://youtu.be/8Q6Q8Qn6L9I. The authors' contact information can be found at the end of this paper. Keywords: Mobile augmented reality, service decomposition, energy efficiency, quality of experience, mobile cloud computing, offloading techniques. 
